summary:
Mispriced Winters is an open-source Python app that shows how a warming climate quietly misprices weather derivatives. Energy companies, farmers and insurers hedge cold winters and hot summers with futures and options on heating and cooling degree days. These contracts are often priced by averaging the last 10–20 seasons, and in a warming climate that average overstates the cold. Answering the Earth System Trend Detective challenge, the app uses NASA POWER daily temperatures since 1981 to show:
- what is changing: seasonal degree-day indices;
- where: 18 cities in the US, Europe and Ukraine, or any point on Earth;
- by how much: the Theil–Sen slope;
- whether it is significant: the Mann–Kendall test with the Hamed–Rao correction, plus Benjamini–Hochberg control across cities.
It then prices the same option three ways: naive burn analysis, a trend-adjusted burn and a Monte Carlo temperature model. A walk-forward backtest that never uses future data checks each price against what actually happened. The result turns a slow climate trend into a number hedgers and traders can act on: how many dollars the naive price is off.
